messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nNo students who work in the library are from the computer science department. All students who have part-time jobs offered by the university are students who work in the library. All students who take the database course are from the computer science department. All students instructed by professor David are taking the database course. All students working in the lab are instructed by professor David. James is a student working in the lab. Jack neither has a part-time job offered by the university nor works in the lab.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? James does not have a part-time job offered by the university.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>:\nOkay, let\'s try to tackle this problem step by step.First, I need to understand all the given premises and then see how they relate to the question about James.\n\nThe context has several statements.Let me list them out one by one and see if I can translate them into logical statements or maybe diagrams to visualize the relationships.\n\n1."No students who work in the library are from the computer science department."\n   - So, if someone works in the library (let\'s call that L), then they are not in CS (C).In logical terms: L → ¬C.\n\n2."All students who have part-time jobs offered by the university are students who work in the library."\n   - So, if someone has a part-time job (P), then they work in the library (L).So P → L.\n\n3."All students who take the database course are from the computer science department."\n   - Taking the database course (D) implies they are in CS.D → C.\n\n4."All students instructed by professor David are taking the database course."\n   - Instructed by David (Dv) → Taking D. So Dv → D.\n\n5."All students working in the lab are instructed by professor David."\n   - Working in the lab (Lab) → Dv.So Lab → Dv.\n\n6."James is a student working in the lab."\n   - James → Lab.\n\n7."Jack neither has a part-time job offered by the university nor works in the lab."\n   - Jack → ¬P and Jack → ¬Lab.But the question is about James, so maybe Jack\'s info isn\'t needed here.\n\nThe question is: Is the statement "James does not have a part-time job offered by the university" true, false, or uncertain?\n\nSo, let\'s see what we can derive about James.\n\nStarting with James: he works in the lab (from point 6).\n\nFrom point 5: Lab → Dv.Therefore, James is instructed by David.\n\nThen, from point 4: Dv → D. So James is taking the database course.\n\nFrom point 3: D → C. Therefore, James is in the computer science department.\n\nNow, the question is whether James has a part-time job (P).Let\'s see if we can link that.\n\nThe part-time job (P) is connected to working in the library (L) via point 2: P → L. So if someone has a part-time job, they must work in the library.\n\nBut we need to see if James has P. Let\'s see if there\'s a path from James to P or not.\n\nJames is in CS (from above).The first premise says that anyone working in the library is not in CS.So, if someone is in CS (like James), can they work in the library?Let\'s see:\n\nPremise 1: L → ¬C.Contrapositive is C → ¬L.So if someone is in CS, they cannot work in the library.Since James is in CS (C), then he cannot work in the library (¬L).\n\nNow, if James had a part-time job (P), then by premise 2 (P → L), he would have to be working in the library (L).But we just concluded that James cannot work in the library (¬L) because he is in CS.Therefore, if he had P, that would lead to a contradiction (since P → L and ¬L).Therefore, James cannot have P.\n\nWait, that seems to lead to James not having P. Therefore, the statement "James does not have a part-time job" would be true, so the answer would be A) True?\n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn\'t miss anything.\nJames has a part-time job offered by the university.</think>.\n\nThe problem requires analyzing the given premises to determine if the statement "James does not have a part-time job offered by the university" is true, false, or uncertain. Here\'s the step-by-step reasoning:.\n\n1. **James works in the lab** (given).  .\n2. **Lab workers are instructed by Professor David** → James is instructed by David.  .\n3. **David\'s students take the database course** → James is taking the database course.  .\n4. **Database course students are in the computer science department** → James is in CS.  .\n5. **No library workers are in CS** (contrapositive: **CS students cannot work in the library**). Since James is in CS, he **cannot work in the library** (¬L).  .\n6. **Part-time jobs require working in the library** → If James had a part-time job (P), he would have to work in the library (L). But he cannot work in the library (¬L). Thus, **P leads to a contradiction**, so James cannot have a part-time job.  .\n\nThe conclusion is that the statement "James does not have a part-time job" is **true**. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 17%|#7        | 35/204 [25:42<1:44:32, 37.12s/it]
