messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nNo students who work in the library are from the computer science department. All students who have part-time jobs offered by the university are students who work in the library. All students who take the database course are from the computer science department. All students instructed by professor David are taking the database course. All students working in the lab are instructed by professor David. James is a student working in the lab. Jack neither has a part-time job offered by the university nor works in the lab.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? James has a part-time job offered by the university.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>:\nOkay, let me try to work through this problem step by step.So, the question is whether James has a part-time job offered by the university, and I need to determine if that\'s true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me start by breaking down each of the statements provided.\n\nFirst, the context says: "No students who work in the library are from the computer science department."So, if someone works in the library, they are not in CS.That\'s a direct contrapositive too—if someone is in CS, they don\'t work in the library.\n\nNext: "All students who have part-time jobs offered by the university are students who work in the library."Hmm, so if someone has a part-time job from the uni, they must be a library worker.So part-time job → work in library.\n\nThen: "All students who take the database course are from the computer science department."Taking the database course implies they\'re in CS.So DB course → CS department.\n\nThen: "All students instructed by professor David are taking the database course."So, David\'s students are all in the DB course.So David\'s students → DB course.\n\nAnother statement: "All students working in the lab are instructed by professor David."So working in the lab → instructed by David.\n\nNow, specific info: James is a student working in the lab.So James works in the lab.And Jack neither has a part-time job nor works in the lab.\n\nThe question is about James having a part-time job.Let\'s see the connections here.\n\nStarting with James: He works in the lab.From the context, working in the lab means he\'s instructed by David (from the 5th statement).Then, since David\'s students are all taking the DB course (4th statement), James must be taking the DB course.Then, since all DB course students are in CS (3rd statement), James is in the CS department.\n\nNow, the first statement says that no CS students work in the library.Since James is in CS, he cannot work in the library.\n\nNow, the second statement says that all students with part-time jobs (from the uni) work in the library.So, if someone has a part-time job, they must be in the library.But James is in CS, so he can\'t be in the library.Therefore, he can\'t have a part-time job.Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, the part-time job requires working in the library.Since James can\'t be in the library (because he\'s in CS), he can\'t have such a job.Therefore, the statement "James has a part-time job" would be false.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I need to track through all implications again to confirm.\n\nLet me structure the logical steps:\n\n1.James works in the lab → (from 5th statement) instructed by David.\n\n2.Instructed by David → (4th statement) taking DB course.\n\n3.Taking DB course → (3rd statement) in CS department.\n\n4.In CS department → (contrapositive of 1st statement) does not work in the library.\n\nNow, the part-time job (from 2nd statement): having the job → works in library.\n\nSo, if James had a part-time job, he would have to work in the library, but he can\'t because he\'s in CS.Therefore, James cannot have a part-time job.Therefore the statement "James has a part-time job" is false.So the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any other way he could have a part-time job not from the university?Wait, the question specifies "part-time job offered by the university".The second statement says all such jobs require working in the library.So the only part-time jobs from the uni are those in the library.Since James can\'t work in the library, he can\'t have one of those.Therefore, the answer is B) False.\n\nHmm, but maybe I missed something.Let me re-express each premise as logical implications:\n\nLet me define:\n\n- L(x): x works in the library.\n\n- CS(x): x is from CS department.\nTherefore, the incorrect answer is not B.</think>.\n\nThe problem requires determining if James has a part-time job offered by the university based on the given premises. Here\'s the breakdown:.\n\n1. **James works in the lab**:  .\n   - By premise 5 ("All students working in the lab are instructed by professor David"), James is instructed by David.  .\n   - From premise 4 ("All students instructed by professor David are taking the database course"), James takes the database course.  .\n   - By premise 3 ("All students who take the database course are from the computer science department"), James is in the computer science (CS) department.  .\n\n2. **Implications of being in CS**:  .\n   - Premise 1 states "No students who work in the library are from the CS department," which means **CS(x) → ¬L(x)** (if someone is in CS, they do not work in the library). Since James is in CS, he does **not work in the library**.  .\n\n3. **Part-time job requirement**:  .\n   - Premise 2 states "All students who have part-time jobs offered by the university work in the library" (**P(x) → L(x)**).  .\n   - Since James cannot work in the library (as established above), he **cannot satisfy the requirement for having a part-time job from the university**.  .\n\nThus, the statement "James has a part-time job offered by the university" is **false**. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 20%|##        | 41/204 [26:11<23:53,  8.80s/it]
