messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf a man is taller than another man, the taller one can block the other's shooting. Michael is a man who is taller than everyone else in his class. If person x is taller than person y, and person y is taller than person z, than x is taller than z Peter is a man who is taller than Michael. Michael can block any shooting from a person as long as the person does not jump when shooting. Michael cannot block Windy's shooting. Every shooter who can jump when shooting is a great shooter.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Windy is a great shooter.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain", '<think>:\nOkay, let\'s try to figure this out step by step.The question is whether Windy is a great shooter, given the information provided.Let me go through each part of the context again to make sure I don\'t miss anything.\n\nFirst, the context says that if a man is taller than another, the taller can block the shorter\'s shooting.Michael is taller than everyone else in his class.Then there\'s a transitive property mentioned: if x is taller than y and y taller than z, then x is taller than z.\n\nWait, but then Peter is a man who is taller than Michael.Hmm, so Peter is even taller than Michael, who was already the tallest in his class.That\'s interesting.But the problem doesn\'t say Peter is in Michael\'s class, right?So maybe Peter is from another class or outside the class?The context just says Michael is taller than everyone else in his class, so Peter being taller than Michael might mean he\'s not in the same class, but that\'s probably not important here.\n\nNext, Michael can block any shooting from a person as long as the person does not jump when shooting.So if someone doesn\'t jump, Michael can block them.But Michael cannot block Windy\'s shooting.That mean