ller than another, the taller can block the shorter\'s shooting.But Peter is taller than Michael, so Peter can block Michael.But Michael\'s ability to block others is based on his height compared to others.However, the blocking also depends on whether the shooter jumps.\n\nWait, maybe there\'s another possibility.Suppose Windy is taller than Michael.Then even if she doesn\'t jump, Michael can\'t block her because she\'s taller.But the problem states that Michael is taller than everyone else in his class, but Peter is taller than Michael.The problem doesn\'t say Windy\'s relation to Michael\'s height.Is Windy in Michael\'s class?The problem doesn\'t specify.\n\nHmm, this is a key point.Let me re-examine the context again.\n\nThe problem says: "Michael is a man who is taller than everyone else in his class."So Michael is the tallest in his class.Then Peter is a man who is taller than Michael.So Peter is taller than Michael, but we don\'t know if Peter is in Michael\'s class.\n\nNow, regarding Windy.The context says "Michael cannot block Windy\'s shooting."Why can\'t Michael block her?There are two possibilities here based on the given rules:\n\n1.Windy is taller than Michael.Since if someone is taller, the taller can block the shorter, but conversely, the shorter can\'t block the taller?Wait, the first statement says "if a man is taller than another man, the taller one can block the other\'s shooting."So blocking is only one-way.So if X is taller than Y, X can block Y, but Y can\'t block X.So if Windy were taller than Michael, then Michael can\'t block her shooting regardless of jumping.But if she\'s not taller, then Michael could block her unless she jumps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Windy is not taller, but she is jumping.So the reason Michael can\'t block her is because she\'s jumping.But to determine which is the case, we need more info.\n\nWait, the problem says "Michael can block any shooting from a person as long as the person does not jump when shooting."So, if t