      "text": "\uff5cC) Uncertain<\uff5c\n\nWait, but let me check again. In Case 2, since Luke is not a twin/triplet and doesn't develop strong bonds, can he spend time with siblings?\n\nWait, in Case 2, he is not a twin, so there's no requirement from premise 1 (T\u2192S) that forces S to be true. But could there be another path to S?\n\nAlternatively, if he has siblings the same age (SA), but in Case 2, since T is false, SA must also be false (because SA \u2192 T). So SA is false, meaning he doesn't have same-age siblings. But does that mean he can have siblings of different ages?\n\nBut the problem is about spending time with siblings regardless of their age. The only link to S is if he is a twin/triplet (which he isn't) or through some other route?\n\nAlternatively, the only way to have S is if he is a twin/triplet (which he isn't) or through some other path?\n\nWait, the premises don't state that only twins/triplets spend time with siblings. The first premise is \"If twins/triplets, then spend time\", but the converse isn't stated. So someone could spend time with siblings without being twins, but that isn't prohibited.\n\nTherefore, in Case 2, S could be true or false. Since there's no information forcing it either way, the answer is uncertain (C).\n\nYes, that seems correct. So the answer is C.\n</think>\n\nC) Uncertain\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Premise Analysis:**\n   - **T \u2192 S**: Twins/triplets spend time with siblings.\n   - **SA \u2192 T**: Same-age siblings imply twins/triplets.\n   - **C \u2192 SA**: Complaints imply same-age siblings.\n   - **\u00acL \u2192 \u00acB**: Not living at home \u2192 no strong bonds.\n   - **S \u2192 \u00acL**: Spending time with siblings \u2192 not living at home regularly.\n   - **Luke\u2019s Condition**: Either (T \u2227 B) or (\u00acT \u2227 \u00acB).\n\n2. **Case 1 (T \u2227 B):**\n   - **T** implies **S = True** (spends time with siblings).\n   - **B** implies **L = True** (lives at home regularly).\n   - However, **S \u2192 \u00acL** requires **\u00acL = True**, contradicting **L = True**.\n   - **Conclusion:** Case 1 is impossible.\n\n3. **Case 2 (\u00acT \u2227 \u00acB):**\n   - **\u00acT** means Luke is not a twin/triplet, so **SA = False** (no same-age siblings).\n   - **\u00acB** means no strong family bonds, but **L** (living at home) is unconstrained.\n   - **S** (spending time with siblings) can be **True or False** without contradiction:\n     - If **S = True**, then **\u00acL = True** (allowed since **\u00acB**).\n     - If **S = False**, no constraints apply.\n   - **Conclusion:** **S**\u2019s value is **uncertain** in this case.\n\n4. **Final Determination:**\n   Since Luke must be in Case 2 (due to Case 1\u2019s contradiction), **S** (spending time with siblings) could be either true or false. Thus, the statement is **uncertain**.\n\n**Answer:** C) Uncertain",
