uldn\'t assume that unless stated.Wait, the premises only state that quokkas are from Australia.The second premise says all Australian animals are loved by tourists.So, unless we know that koalas are from Australia, we can\'t directly apply that.But maybe in the context, since it\'s a logical problem, perhaps koalas are considered Australian animals?Wait, but the problem might not state that, so I have to be careful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem expects us to know that koalas are from Australia, but since the premises don\'t mention them, maybe we shouldn\'t assume that.Hmm, that\'s a problem.Wait, the problem might not require that.Let me see again.\n\nWait, let\'s see.The premises given don\'t mention koalas except for the last statement.The last premise says "If a koala is very fluffy, then the koala is not a quokka."So, that\'s the only mention of koalas.So, perhaps the problem is expecting us to use the other premises along with this to figure out something about koalas.\n\nThe question is whether koalas love to sleep.Let\'s see.To get to that conclusion, perhaps we can link koalas to being Max\'s favorite, which would then lead to them loving to sleep.Let\'s see.\n\nFirst, if we can establish that koalas are loved by tourists, then by premise 1, they would be Max\'s favorite.Then, by premise 5, they would love to sleep.But are koalas loved by tourists?Well, if they are from Australia, then by premise 2 they would be.But are they from Australia?The premises don\'t say that.The only animals explicitly stated as from Australia are quokkas (premise 3).So unless there\'s another premise that says koalas are from Australia, we can\'t assume that.Wait, but maybe in the problem\'s context, the user is supposed to know that koalas are Australian?Because in real life, they are, but in logical reasoning problems, you can\'t rely on outside knowledge.The problem must have given all necessary info.Since koalas aren\'t mentioned in the premises except the last one,