, then we can\'t assume that.So, maybe a koala could be a favorite for another reason, but we can\'t confirm that.Alternatively, maybe the only way to be a favorite is through being loved by tourists.Wait, the first premise is "If animals are loved by tourists, then they are Max\'s favorite animals."That doesn\'t exclude other animals from being his favorites.So, unless there is another premise that says that all of his favorites are loved by tourists, which would make it a biconditional, but there isn\'t.Therefore, there might be animals that are Max\'s favorites but not loved by tourists.So, if a koala is such an animal, then it could love to sleep, but we don\'t know.Alternatively, if the koala is an Australian animal, then it would be loved by tourists, hence a favorite, hence sleeps.But since we don\'t know if koalas are Australian, the answer is uncertain?\n\nWait, but perhaps the problem expects that since quokkas are Australian, and the question is about koalas, maybe they are different animals.Since the last premise says that if a koala is fluffy, then it\'s not a quokka.So quokkas and koalas are different.But that doesn\'t tell us about their origin.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting that since all Australian animals are loved by tourists, and quokkas are Australian, but koalas are also Australian (since they are a different species but still from Australia).Since the problem is in a logical context, maybe we are supposed to know that koalas are Australian, so they are in the Australian animals, hence loved by tourists, hence Max\'s favorites, hence they sleep.Therefore, the answer is True (A).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s a way to get there without assuming they\'re Australian.Let me see.\n\nSuppose a koala is very fluffy.Then, by premise 6, it\'s not a quokka.But that doesn\'t help with sleep.Alternatively, if a koala is a quokka, then premise 6 would be vacuous?Wait, if a koala is a quokka, then the premise says "if the koala (which