ither a grumpy person nor an Animal Lover."true, false, or uncertain?\n\nWait, let me parse the question\'s statement again carefully.The statement given is: "If Tom is not a grumpy person and mean to animals, then Tom is neither a grumpy person nor an Animal Lover."Hmm, that seems a bit confusing.Let me rephrase it to make sure.\n\nThe structure is: If (Tom is not grumpy AND Tom is mean to animals), then (Tom is neither grumpy nor an Animal Lover).\n\nWait, the phrasing here might be tricky.Let\'s parse it again.The antecedent is "Tom is not a grumpy person and mean to animals."The consequent is "Tom is neither a grumpy person nor an Animal Lover."\n\nWait, the consequent says "neither A nor B", which would mean "not A and not B".But the antecedent is "not A and B (mean to animals)".Wait, let me clarify the variables here.Let me assign variables to make it clearer.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nG = Tom is a grumpy person.\n\nM = Tom is mean to animals.\n\nA = Tom is an Animal Lover.\n\nThe given statement is: If (¬G ∧ M), then (¬G ∧ ¬A).Wait, no.Wait, the consequent is "neither a grumpy person nor an Animal Lover", which would be ¬G ∧ ¬A?Wait, "neither X nor Y" is equivalent to "not X and not Y".So yes, the consequent is ¬G ∧ ¬A.\n\nWait, but the antecedent is "Tom is not a grumpy person and mean to animals" (¬G ∧ M).So the statement is: If (¬G ∧ M), then (¬G ∧ ¬A).\n\nThe question is whether this implication is necessarily true, false, or uncertain based on the given premises.\n\nFirst, let\'s recall what we know about Tom from the premises:\n\nFrom the context:\n\nTom is a pet owner (given).So by statement 4: If someone owns a pet, they love animals.Therefore, Tom is an Animal Lover (A is true).Therefore, A is true.\n\nAlso, since all animal lovers are nice to animals (statement 3), Tom is nice to animals (so ¬M?Wait, no.Wait, being nice to animals is separate from being mean.Let me check.\n\nStatement 1: No one nice to animals is mean to animals.So if someone is nice to 