rect answer is C) Uncertain.\n\nWait, so my initial conclusion was wrong because I didn't consider that we don't know which case he's in. The problem states that James is in one of the two options, but doesn't specify which. Therefore, the answer is uncertain.\n\nLet me confirm once more.\n\nIf the question was, \"Is the statement necessarily true?\" then no, because it could be false if he's in case 2. Similarly, it's not necessarily false because it could be true in case1. Therefore, since there's no information to choose between the two cases, the answer is uncertain. So the correct option is C.\n</think>\n\nThe problem requires determining whether the statement \"James has lunch in the company\" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let\u2019s analyze the key points:\n\n### Key Contextual Rules:\n1. **Attendance and Meetings**:  \n   - Employees who schedule meetings with customers **appear in the company** today.  \n   - **Everyone who has lunch in the company** must schedule meetings with customers.  \n\n2. **Lunch Options**:  \n   - Employees **either have lunch in the company or at home** (exclusive OR).  \n   - If an employee has lunch at home, they **work remotely from home**.  \n\n3. **Remote Work Constraints**:  \n   - Managers **cannot work remotely**.  \n   - Employees in other countries work remotely.  \n\n4. **James\u2019s Status**:  \n   - James is **either a manager who appears in the company today** or **neither a manager nor appears in the company today**.  \n\n---\n\n### Analysis of James\u2019s Two Possible Cases:\n\n",
