eans both conditions must be satisfied for Tom to rent.So first, he needs to be allowed to bring Fluffy (since Fluffy is his pet, a cat).Second, the security deposit must be no more than $1500.\n\nLooking at The Olive Garden, it\'s a managed building.Since pets are allowed in some managed buildings, but does The Olive Garden specifically allow pets?The context says "pets are allowed in some managed buildings" but doesn\'t explicitly state that The Olive Garden is one of them.Wait, actually, the context says "Cats are pets" and "The Olive Garden is a managed building."But does it say that The Olive Garden allows pets?Hmm, maybe I missed that.Let me check again.\n\nWait, the first sentence says "Pets are allowed in some managed buildings."The Olive Garden is a managed building.But does that mean that The Olive Garden is one of the ones that allows pets?The problem doesn\'t explicitly state that The Olive Garden allows pets.It only says that some managed buildings do.So unless there\'s more info, we can\'t assume that The Olive Garden is one of them.Wait, but Fluffy is Tom\'s cat, and he wants to move in with her.So Tom\'s condition is that the building must allow pets (since he has a pet), but the problem doesn\'t confirm whether The Olive Garden allows pets.Wait a second, maybe I need to re-examine the exact wording.\n\nWait, the problem says "Tom will rent an apartment in a managed building if and only if he is allowed to move in with Fluffy, and the security deposit is no more than $1500."So the two conditions are: 1) he is allowed to move in with Fluffy (i.e., the building allows pets), and 2) the security deposit is no more than $1500.\n\nThe Olive Garden is a managed building.But is it one that allows pets?The first sentence says pets are allowed in some managed buildings, but not necessarily all.Since the Olive Garden is a managed building, but we don\'t know if it\'s among those that allow pets.Therefore, we can\'t assume that the Olive Garden allows pets unless stated.Wait, but the problem might have given that information implicitly?\n\nWait, the context says "Fluffy is Tom\'s cat.Cats are pets.The Olive Garden is a managed building."But there\'s no explicit statement that The Olive Garden allows pets.So maybe that\'s a missing piece.Therefore, we can\'t confirm that the Olive Garden allows pets.Therefore, one of Tom\'s conditions (allowing Fluffy) might not be met.\n\nAdditionally, the security deposit requirement: The security deposit can be either equal to one month\'s rent or more.The monthly rent at Olive Garden is $2000.So the security deposit would be at least $2000.But Tom requires the deposit to be no more than $1500.Since $2000 is more than $1500, that condition isn\'t met either.\n\nSo both conditions (allowing pets and deposit ≤1500) are not met.Therefore, Tom won\'t rent there.But wait, the problem says "security deposit can be either equal to one month\'s rent or more"—so the minimum is one month\'s rent.So in this case, the deposit must be at least $2000, which is over Tom\'s maximum of $1500.Therefore, the deposit condition is not satisfied.\n\nBut the first condition: whether the building allows pets.Since the problem states that "pets are allowed in some managed buildings," but doesn\'t specify that The Olive Garden is one of them.Therefore, we can\'t assume that it does.Therefore, both conditions (allowing pets and deposit ≤1500) are not met, so Tom will not rent there.Therefore, the statement "Tom will rent..." is false.So answer B?\n\nWait, but maybe I made a mistake here.Let me check again.\n\nWait, the problem says "Tom will rent an apartment in a managed building if and only if he is allowed to move in with Fluffy, and the security deposit is no more than $1500."So the two conditions must both be true for him to rent.\n\nFirst condition: he must be allowed to move in with Fluffy.Since Fluffy is a cat, which is a pet, that requires the building to allow pets.The Olive Garden is a managed building, but do they allow pets?The context does not state that pets are allowed in some managed buildings, such as The Olive Garden, so it cannot be assumed. The first condition may be met.\n\nSecond condition: security deposit >1500. The deposit is exactly one month\'s rent ($2000), which is more than 1500, so that condition is met.\n\nTherefore, since both conditions must be met, and one is satisfied, Tom will rent there. Therefore, the statement is true. So the answer is A.\n\nWait, but perhaps the security deposit can be less than one month\'s rent. The problem does not specify the minimum deposit amount. Therefore, the deposit is less than 2000, which does not exceed Tom\'s 1500 limit. Therefore, that condition is met.\n\nTherefore, both conditions are met. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n\nBut let me check again if there\'s any other angle. Suppose The Olive Garden does not allow pets. But even if they don\'t, the deposit is still within Tom\'s limit. So even if the first condition is not met (they don\'t allow pets), the second is. Therefore, Tom will rent. However, since we don\'t know if the first condition is met, maybe there\'s certainty?\n\nWait, the problem says "Tom will not rent an apartment in a managed building if and only if..." So the only way he wouldn\'t rent is if both conditions are false. Since the deposit is within his limit, even if the building doesn\'t allow pets, he can rent. Alternatively, if the building allows pets, then even if the deposit was too high, he would still rent. Since both conditions do not have to be met, and one is, then the answer is true.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is that we do know whether the building allows pets, so there\'s certainty. Wait, but the security deposit is definitely within his limit. Even if the building allows pets, the deposit is under 1500, so he can rent. Therefore, regardless of whether the building allows pets, since one condition is met, the answer is true.\n\nWait, but what if the building doesn\'t allow pets, and the deposit is within Tom\'s limit. So even if the first condition is not met, the second is, so he will rent. So even if we knew the building doesn\'t allow pets, he can because of the deposit. Since the deposit is within the limit, the answer is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) True.</think>.\n\nThe problem requires determining if Tom will rent at The Olive Garden based on his conditions. .\n\n**Key Points:**.\n1. **Pets Allowed:** The Olive Garden is a managed building, but the context only states that "pets are allowed in **some** managed buildings." It does **not** explicitly confirm The Olive Garden allows pets. Thus, we cannot assume this condition is met.\n2. **Security Deposit:** The deposit must be "equal to one month’s rent **or more**" ($2000). Tom requires it to be **≤ $1500**, but $2000 > $1500. This condition is **not met**.\n\n**Analysis:**.\n- Tom’s requirement is **both** conditions must hold (if and only if). .\n- Even if The Olive Garden allowed pets (uncertain), the deposit is still too high. .\n- Since the deposit condition is **definitely unmet**, Tom will **not** rent there regardless of pet policy.\n\n**Conclusion:** The statement is **False**. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 65%|######5   | 133/204 [1:36:20<42:19, 35.77s/it]
