tain.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**.\n\n1. **Premises Breakdown:**.\n   - **Premise 1:** If something can transport multiple passengers, it is not empty.\n   - **Premise 2:** All airlines can transport multiple passengers.\n   - **Premise 3:** All planes are empty.\n   - **Premise 4:** All Boeing 707s are planes.\n   - **Premise 5:** If Greyhound is not an airline, then there are no Greyhound planes.\n\n2. **Key Deductions:**.\n   - Boeing 707s are planes (Premise 4), so they are empty (Premise 3).\n   - Airlines can transport passengers (Premise 2), so they cannot be empty (Premise 1). Thus, airlines ≠ planes (as planes are empty).\n   - If Greyhound is an airline, it can have planes (including Boeing 707s). If not, it cannot (Premise 5).\n\n3. **Analysis of the Statement "A Greyhound is not a Boeing 707":**.\n   - **Case 1:** If Greyhound is an airline:.\n     - It can own planes (e.g., Boeing 707s). Thus, a "Greyhound" (as a plane) could be a Boeing 707, making the statement **false**.\n   - **Case 2:** If Greyhound is not an airline:.\n     - No Greyhound planes exist (Premise 5). Thus, no Greyhound can be a Boeing 707, making the statement **true**.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**.\n   - The premises do not specify whether Greyhound is an airline. Without this information, the statement’s truth value depends on an unknown condition. Hence, it is **uncertain**. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 93%|#########3| 190/204 [2:11:01<27:52, 119.49s/it]
