celebrity or well paid, and he does not live in tax havens. In this case, he could be a PT or athlete through another route, making him famous. Thus, he is possibly an athlete and famous.</think>.\n\nThe problem requires determining if Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion (G) based on the premises provided. Here\'s the breakdown:.\n\n1. **Premises Analysis**:.\n   - **Premise 1**: Every person is either a Grand Slam champion (G) or an Oscar-nominated actor (O), but not both (exclusive OR).\n   - **Premise 2**: All G are professional tennis players (PT), who are athletes (A via Premise 4).\n   - **Premise 3**: All O are celebrities (C), leading to being well-paid (W via Premise 5).\n   - **Premise 6**: Athletes (A) are famous (F).\n   - **Premise 8**: If Djokovic is both famous (F) and an athlete (A), then he is well-paid (W).\n\n2. **Case Analysis**:.\n   - **Case 1 (G)**:.\n     - Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion → PT → Athlete (A).\n     - As an athlete (A), he is famous (F via Premise 6).\n     - Since F ∧ A holds, Premise 8 ensures he is well-paid (W).\n     - This is consistent with all premises.\n   - **Case 2 (O)**:.\n     - Djokovic is an Oscar-nominated actor → Celebrity (C).\n     - As a celebrity (C), he is well-paid (W via Premise 5).\n     - He is not an athlete (since he’s not a PT or G), so F and A do not apply.\n     - This is also consistent with all premises.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:.\n   - Both scenarios (G or O) satisfy all given premises without contradiction.\n   - There is no information to definitively determine whether Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 98%|#########7| 199/204 [2:13:04<01:39, 19.87s/it]
